### What version of Codex CLI is running?

[Codex 0.155.1](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.155.1), the same issue remains in [Codex CLI prerelease 0.157.0-alpha.1](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.157.0-alpha.1).

### What subscription do you have?

Not applicable, no account was used.

### Which model were you using?

Not applicable, a local test server returned fixed responses, no actual model was used.

### What platform is your computer?

NixOS 26.05.

### What terminal emulator and version are you using (if applicable)?

Not applicable, the script communicates directly with the app-server over WebSocket.

### Codex doctor report

Not collected for the isolated reproduction.

### What issue are you seeing?

Codex can report a successfully completed turn as interrupted and omit its final answer when a client resumes the conversation during completion. The answer is still saved: a subsequent `thread/read` returns it correctly.

This happens when the turn finishes after the server reads history for the resuming client but before that client starts receiving live updates. The completion is missing from both, so a reconnecting user could be prompted to repeat work that already succeeded.

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

Use the [Codex 0.155.1 source](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.155.1), save the two blocks below as `resume-timing.patch` and `reproduce_resume.mjs`. Apply the patch from the checkout root and build the app-server:

```bash
git apply --check /absolute/path/to/resume-timing.patch
git apply /absolute/path/to/resume-timing.patch
cd codex-rs
cargo build -p codex-app-server --bin codex-app-server
```

Run the script with the absolute path to the newly built `codex-app-server` executable:

```bash
node /absolute/path/to/reproduce_resume.mjs /absolute/path/to/codex-app-server
```

The script needs no npm packages, it creates temporary test homes and a local model endpoint, then runs a control followed by the overlapping case.

<details>
<summary>Reproduction timing patch</summary>

```diff
--- a/codex-rs/app-server/src/request_processors/thread_processor.rs
+++ b/codex-rs/app-server/src/request_processors/thread_processor.rs
@@ -4472,6 +4472,22 @@
             };
             let resume_cursor_store = paginated_resume.then(|| Arc::clone(&self.thread_store));

+            // Reproduction timing hook, enabled only by a fixture-owned file.
+            // All normal history reads are complete; no stored values are changed.
+            let resume_gate = self.config.codex_home.join("resume-gate");
+            if resume_gate.exists() {
+                tokio::fs::write(resume_gate.with_extension("captured"), b"captured")
+                    .await
+                    .expect("write capture marker");
+                tokio::time::timeout(Duration::from_secs(30), async {
+                    while !resume_gate.with_extension("release").exists() {
+                        tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(5)).await;
+                    }
+                })
+                .await
+                .expect("release resume within 30 seconds");
+            }
+
             let (completion_tx, completion_rx) = tokio::sync::oneshot::channel();
             let command = crate::thread_state::ThreadListenerCommand::SendThreadResumeResponse {
                 request: Box::new(crate::thread_state::PendingThreadResumeRequest {
```

</details>

<details>
<summary>Standalone WebSocket reproduction</summary>

```javascript
import http from 'node:http';
import net from 'node:net';
import fs from 'node:fs/promises';
import os from 'node:os';
import path from 'node:path';
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process';
import { setTimeout as sleep } from 'node:timers/promises';

// Run against the app-server built with resume-timing.patch.
const binary = process.argv[2];
if (!binary) {
  throw new Error('Usage: node reproduce_resume.mjs /path/to/codex-app-server');
}

// By default, run the before-resume control, then complete during paused resume.
const trials = JSON.parse(process.env.RESUME_DELAYS || '[-1,0]');
const seedBytes = Number(process.env.RESUME_SEED_BYTES || 1000);
const mode = process.env.RESUME_HISTORY_MODE || 'legacy';
const gated = process.env.RESUME_GATED !== '0';
const page = process.env.RESUME_PAGE === '1';
const outcome = process.env.RESUME_OUTCOME || 'completed';

const home = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'resume-home-'));
const project = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'resume-project-'));
let fixtureResponses = [];

function sseEvent(type, data) {
  return `event: ${type}\ndata: ${JSON.stringify({ type, ...data })}\n\n`;
}

function finalEvents(text, id) {
  return sseEvent('response.output_item.added', {
    output_index: 0,
    item: {
      type: 'message', id, role: 'assistant', content: [], phase: 'final_answer',
    },
  }) + sseEvent('response.output_item.done', {
    output_index: 0,
    item: {
      type: 'message', id, role: 'assistant',
      content: [{ type: 'output_text', text, annotations: [] }],
      phase: 'final_answer',
    },
  }) + sseEvent('response.completed', {
    response: {
      id: 'resp-' + id,
      status: 'completed',
      output: [],
      usage: { input_tokens: 1, output_tokens: 1, total_tokens: 2 },
    },
  });
}

// Hold each model response open until the test chooses when to finish it.
const model = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') {
    res.writeHead(404);
    res.end();
    return;
  }
  for await (const _ of req) {}
  res.writeHead(200, {
    'content-type': 'text/event-stream',
    'connection': 'keep-alive',
  });
  res.write(sseEvent('response.created', {
    response: { id: 'resp-fixture', status: 'in_progress' },
  }));
  fixtureResponses.push(res);
});
await new Promise(resolve => model.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', resolve));

// Find a free local port for the app-server's WebSocket listener.
const holder = net.createServer();
await new Promise(resolve => holder.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', resolve));
const port = holder.address().port;
await new Promise(resolve => holder.close(resolve));

await fs.writeFile(path.join(home, 'config.toml'), `model = "mock-model"
model_provider = "fixture"
approval_policy = "never"
sandbox_mode = "read-only"
check_for_update_on_startup = false
[model_providers.fixture]
name = "Resume local fixture"
base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:${model.address().port}/v1"
wire_api = "responses"
request_max_retries = 0
stream_max_retries = 0
`);

const child = spawn(binary, ['--listen', `ws://127.0.0.1:${port}`], {
  env: { ...process.env, CODEX_HOME: home },
  stdio: ['ignore', 'ignore', 'pipe'],
});
let stderr = '';
child.stderr.on('data', data => { stderr += data.toString(); });
const clients = [];
let seq = 0;

class Rpc {
  constructor(ws) {
    this.ws = ws;
    this.messages = [];
    this.waiters = [];
    ws.onmessage = event => {
      const message = JSON.parse(event.data);
      this.messages.push(message);
      for (const waiter of [...this.waiters]) {
        if (waiter.predicate(message)) {
          this.waiters.splice(this.waiters.indexOf(waiter), 1);
          clearTimeout(waiter.timer);
          waiter.resolve(message);
        }
      }
    };
  }

  wait(predicate, timeoutMs = 15000) {
    const existing = this.messages.find(predicate);
    if (existing) return Promise.resolve(existing);
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const waiter = { predicate, resolve };
      waiter.timer = setTimeout(() => {
        this.waiters = this.waiters.filter(item => item !== waiter);
        reject(new Error(
          'RPC wait timeout; recent=' +
          JSON.stringify(this.messages.slice(-5)).slice(0, 2000),
        ));
      }, timeoutMs);
      this.waiters.push(waiter);
    });
  }

  async call(method, params) {
    const id = ++seq;
    this.ws.send(JSON.stringify({ id, method, params }));
    const message = await this.wait(item => item.id === id);
    if (message.error) throw new Error(method + ':' + JSON.stringify(message.error));
    return message.result;
  }
}

async function connect() {
  let ws;
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 100; attempt++) {
    try {
      ws = await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const socket = new WebSocket(`ws://127.0.0.1:${port}`);
        socket.onopen = () => resolve(socket);
        socket.onerror = reject;
      });
      break;
    } catch {
      await sleep(30);
    }
  }
  if (!ws) throw new Error('server not listening: ' + stderr);
  const client = new Rpc(ws);
  clients.push(client);
  await client.call('initialize', {
    clientInfo: { name: 'resume_fixture', title: 'Resume fixture', version: '1' },
    capabilities: { experimentalApi: true },
  });
  ws.send(JSON.stringify({ method: 'initialized', params: {} }));
  return client;
}

async function waitForModelRequest() {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 1500; attempt++) {
    if (fixtureResponses.length) return fixtureResponses.shift();
    await sleep(10);
  }
  throw new Error('no model request; ' + stderr.slice(-3000));
}

try {
  const primary = await connect();
  const resuming = await connect();
  const results = [];

  for (const delay of trials) {
    const { thread } = await primary.call('thread/start', {
      cwd: project, model: 'mock-model', historyMode: mode,
    });
    const id = thread.id;

    // Complete a seed turn so the conversation already has saved history.
    if (seedBytes) {
      const seed = await primary.call('turn/start', {
        threadId: id,
        input: [{ type: 'text', text: 'seed synthetic history' }],
      });
      const stream = await waitForModelRequest();
      stream.end(finalEvents('S'.repeat(seedBytes), 'seed-' + seq));
      await primary.wait(message =>
        message.method === 'turn/completed' && message.params?.turn?.id === seed.turn.id);
    }

    const started = await primary.call('turn/start', {
      threadId: id,
      input: [{ type: 'text', text: 'finish fixture turn' }],
    });
    const stream = await waitForModelRequest();
    const finalText = 'resume-final-' + started.turn.id;
    const finishTurn = () => outcome === 'interrupted'
      ? primary.call('turn/interrupt', { threadId: id, turnId: started.turn.id })
      : stream.end(finalEvents(finalText, 'final-' + seq));

    // Control: complete before the second client requests history.
    if (delay < 0) {
      await finishTurn();
      await primary.wait(message =>
        message.method === 'turn/completed' && message.params?.turn?.id === started.turn.id);
    }

    if (gated) {
      for (const name of ['resume-gate.captured', 'resume-gate.release']) {
        await fs.rm(path.join(home, name), { force: true });
      }
      await fs.writeFile(path.join(home, 'resume-gate'), 'enabled');
    }
    const before = resuming.messages.length;
    const resumePromise = resuming.call('thread/resume', {
      threadId: id,
      excludeTurns: page,
      ...(page ? { initialTurnsPage: { limit: 10, sortDirection: 'desc' } } : {}),
    });

    if (gated) {
      // Wait for the patched server to finish reading history and pause.
      for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
        if (await fs.stat(path.join(home, 'resume-gate.captured')).catch(() => false)) break;
        if (attempt > 2000) throw new Error('resume did not reach capture barrier');
        await sleep(5);
      }
      if (delay >= 0) {
        await finishTurn();
        await primary.wait(message =>
          message.method === 'turn/completed' && message.params?.turn?.id === started.turn.id);
      }
      await fs.writeFile(path.join(home, 'resume-gate.release'), 'release');
    } else if (delay >= 0) {
      await sleep(delay);
      await finishTurn();
    }

    const resumed = await resumePromise;
    const terminal = await primary.wait(message =>
      message.method === 'turn/completed' && message.params?.turn?.id === started.turn.id);
    await sleep(100);

    const turn = (page ? resumed.initialTurnsPage.data : resumed.thread.turns)
      .find(item => item.id === started.turn.id);
    const hasFinal = JSON.stringify(turn).includes(finalText);
    const streamCompleted = resuming.messages.slice(before).some(message =>
      message.method === 'turn/completed' && message.params?.turn?.id === started.turn.id);
    const streamFinal = resuming.messages.slice(before).some(message =>
      message.method === 'item/completed' && JSON.stringify(message).includes(finalText));
    // Initial pages deliberately omit item bodies; check terminal status there.
    const missed = (outcome === 'completed' && !page && !(hasFinal || streamFinal)) ||
      !(turn?.status === outcome || streamCompleted);

    const fresh = await resuming.call('thread/read', { threadId: id, includeTurns: true });
    const freshTurn = fresh.thread.turns.find(item => item.id === started.turn.id);
    const row = {
      delay, seedBytes, mode, page, gated, outcome,
      turnId: started.turn.id,
      primaryStatus: terminal.params.turn.status,
      resumeStatus: turn?.status,
      hasFinal, streamCompleted, streamFinal, missed,
      freshStatus: freshTurn?.status,
      freshHasFinal: JSON.stringify(freshTurn).includes(finalText),
    };
    row.evidence = {
      resumedTurn: turn,
      primaryTerminal: terminal,
      secondTerminalMessages: resuming.messages.slice(before).filter(message =>
        (message.method === 'turn/completed' || message.method === 'item/completed') &&
        JSON.stringify(message).includes(started.turn.id)),
      refreshedTurn: freshTurn,
    };
    results.push(row);
    console.log(JSON.stringify(row));
  }

  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    summary: {
      trials: results.length,
      missed: results.filter(result => result.missed).length,
      binary, home, mode,
    },
  }));
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error.stack);
  console.error(stderr.slice(-6000));
  process.exitCode = 1;
} finally {
  for (const client of clients) client.ws.close();
  child.kill('SIGTERM');
  for (const stream of fixtureResponses) stream.end();
  model.closeAllConnections();
  await new Promise(resolve => model.close(resolve));
  await fs.writeFile(path.join(home, 'server.stderr.log'), stderr);
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ fixtureHome: home }));
}
```

</details>

For the control (`delay: -1`), expect `resumeStatus: "completed"`, `hasFinal: true`, and `missed: false`. With the bug present, the overlapping case (`delay: 0`) has:

```text
primaryStatus: completed
resumeStatus: interrupted
hasFinal: false
streamCompleted: false
streamFinal: false
missed: true
freshStatus: completed
freshHasFinal: true
```

The script observes notifications for 100 ms before reading the thread again.

The controlled test reproduced the failure with both history modes and a limited initial page; all controls passed.

### What is the expected behavior?

If a turn completes during resume, its completed status and final answer should appear in the returned history or arrive through live updates. The client should not be left with an interrupted turn when the operation succeeded.

### Additional information

#### Versions tested

Reproduced using [Codex 0.155.1 source](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.155.1) with the timing patch. The same history-read/subscription ordering remains in the most recent [Codex CLI prerelease 0.157.0-alpha.1](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.157.0-alpha.1).

#### Root cause analysis

1. [`resume_running_thread()`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/be2951ea34f0d295ed0becf97079f92fa5f6950e/codex-rs/app-server/src/request_processors/thread_processor.rs#L4259) reads history before enqueueing `SendThreadResumeResponse`. The second client has not yet subscribed, so this snapshot can still show the turn in progress.
2. The existing turn can finish while resume is preparing its response. The listener delivers completion to the original client, and [`track_current_turn_event()`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/be2951ea34f0d295ed0becf97079f92fa5f6950e/codex-rs/app-server/src/thread_state.rs#L180) clears the active-turn history.
3. [`handle_pending_thread_resume_request()`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/be2951ea34f0d295ed0becf97079f92fa5f6950e/codex-rs/app-server/src/request_processors/thread_lifecycle.rs#L572) receives the earlier snapshot. It normally supplements that history with the active turn, but the completed turn is no longer in the active snapshot. Since the thread is now idle, it marks the stale in-progress turn as interrupted.
4. The second client subscribes after the final items and completion have already been delivered. Replaying pending server requests does not replay those past notifications, so neither the response nor the live updates supply the missing result.

A later `thread/read` works because the saved history already contains the completed turn and final answer.

#### Suggested fix

I'd suggest coordinating the history read with subscription so that updates arriving between them are retained and delivered. If subscription happens first, the response and buffered updates must still be ordered so that an older snapshot can't overwrite a newer result.

#### Related issues

[#37577](https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/37577) also reports missing content and interrupted turns on resume. Both issues use the same [status handling](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/be2951ea34f0d295ed0becf97079f92fa5f6950e/codex-rs/app-server/src/request_processors/thread_lifecycle.rs#L906-L921), which marks unfinished turns as interrupted when the thread is inactive. Here, the incomplete history comes from a snapshot taken before completion; the cause in #37577 is not found.
